1. Single-Page Application (SPA) vs. Multi-Page Application (MPA)SPA (Single-Page Application): A web application that loads a single HTML page and dynamically updates the page content as the user interacts with the app, without requiring full page reloads from the server.MPA (Multi-Page Application): Traditional web applications where every new view or data change triggers a full browser reload to fetch a completely new HTML page from the server.FeatureSingle-Page Application (SPA)Multi-Page Application (MPA)Page ReloadsRewrites current page without reloadingReloads the full page on every navigationPerformanceFaster after initial load (renders client-side)Slower transition between pagesUser ExperienceFluid, app-like experienceTraditional website feel with page blinksDevelopmentClear separation between front-end & back-endFront-end and back-end are tightly coupledSEOHarder to optimize out of the boxEasier to optimize for search enginesPros & Cons of SPAsPros: Fast user experience, reusable UI components, smooth transitions, easy local caching.Cons: Slower initial page load, SEO challenges without Server-Side Rendering (SSR), relies heavily on client-side JavaScript.2. What is React and How Does It Work?React is an open-source JavaScript library developed by Meta (Facebook) for building user interfaces, primarily for single-page applications.How it Works: React breaks the UI down into reusable, isolated pieces of code called Components. It uses a declarative approach—you describe what the UI should look like for a given state, and React automatically manages updating and rendering the right components when your data changes.Key Features of ReactComponent-Based Architecture: Build UI using modular, reusable building blocks.JSX (JavaScript XML): A syntax extension that lets you write HTML-like structure directly inside JavaScript code.One-Way Data Flow: Data flows down from parent components to child components via props, making code predictable and easier to debug.Virtual DOM: High-performance updates (explained below).3. What is the Virtual DOM?The Virtual DOM (VDOM) is a lightweight, in-memory representation of the actual Real DOM tree.When state changes occur in a React application:React creates a new Virtual DOM tree representing the updated UI.React compares the new Virtual DOM with the previous Virtual DOM snapshot (a process called Reconciliation or Diffing).React calculates the minimal set of changes needed and updates only those specific parts in the actual browser DOM, making rendering significantly faster.